-----------------------------------------------------------------------

This library has Two variations:
A lib That is named OGL
This current version for C: ORCLL. btw!,
ORCLL stands for: (Open-Reach's C Language Library)

and:
A lib That is named OGLP
The version for C++: ORCPPLL. btw!,
ORCPPLL stands for: (Open-Reach's C++ Language Library)

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
They are different but the same messages:
ORCLL uses GTK,
ORCPPLL uses GLFW with GLAD.
-------Why?-------:
I use GLAD because it is what I learned GLFW (OpenGL FrameWork) with,
it is pretty easy to use so thats why I use it instead of GLEW.

ORCLL is meant for small projects (2D apps, and 2D games),
ORCPPLL is meant for advanced projects (3D graphics, and high performance games).

ORCLL is recommended for people who have C knowledge but are in to other stuff than, complex games
ORCPPLL on the other hand is meant for People who have both C and C++ knowledge and know how to use both:
OGL for C (ORCLL) and,
OGLP for C++ (ORCPPLL).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
I want it to be easier for everybody to make compiler configuration files,
to make this true I made OLconfig.txt

These are files you use with your compiler: ORCLL: clang, gcc, etc. / ORCPPLL: clang++, g++, etc.
let's write some text code:
This is inspired by glsl

but these are still in development

#core -: version "*"
#import -: "core"

func main(*):
    return #fmt.start;

;;; For C users of ORCLL
$if: OCLL::Folder.CallInclude("C".lang)->@INCLUDED
    #.proj::core::include -: "/Users/yourname/OGL_ORCLL-1.0/**"; ;;; add the include directory
    #.core::file:*header*:exec -: "/Users/yourname/OGL_ORCLL-1.0/ogl.c"; ;;; The runable file has to be specified
$endif

;;; for C++ users of ORCPPLL
$if: OGCPPLL::Folder.CallInclude("C++".lang)->@INCLUDED
    #.proj::core::include -: "/Users/yourname/OGL_ORCPPLL-1.0/**"; ;;; add the include directory 
    #.core::file:*header*:exec -: "/Users/yourname/OGL_ORCLL-1.0/ogl.cpp"; ;;; add the run file
$endif

for some test code for C (ORCLL):

#include <OGL/ogl1.h>

// fn is a alias for void and static_fn is a alias for static void
// GTK gives windows a automatic size
fn init(olApp* app, olPtr data)
{
    olWidget* window = new_olWindow(app);
    olSetWindowTitle(window, "my app");
    olSetWindowSize(window, 400, 400);

    olShow(window, True);
}

// This automatically stores: int argc, char** argv
ogl_main
{
    you can also store olSignal in a olInt
    olApp* app = new_olApp("com.example.ID.ogl");

    olSignal(app, init, OL_ACTIVATE);

    // ret is a alias for return:
    ret olAppRun(O_APP(app), argc, argv);
}